## Summary

`scripts/publish-to-npm.mjs` treats "publish succeeded" and "verification succeeded" as a single retryable unit. When the publish itself works but the post-publish verification misses because npm's registry has not propagated yet, the retry loop re-runs the **entire** `changeset publish`. The republish then legitimately fails with "cannot publish over the previously published version", so a fully successful release can be reported as a failure — or succeed only by luck, if a later attempt happens to fit inside the retry budget.

Found while auditing the same failure mode downstream in `link-assistant/hive-mind` ([issue #2082](https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/2082)), where it was observed in a real, **green** release run.

## Where

`scripts/publish-to-npm.mjs`

`attemptPublish()` verifies after a fixed 2-second sleep, and returns a plain failure if the version is not visible yet:

```js
console.log('Verifying package was published to npm...');
await sleep(2000); // Wait for npm registry to propagate
const isPublished = await verifyPublished(packageName, currentVersion);

if (isPublished) {
  return { success: true, error: null };
}

console.error('Verification failed: package not found on npm after publish');
return {
  success: false,
  error: new Error('Package not found on npm after publish attempt'),
};
```

That error is not marked `nonRetryable`, so `main()`'s loop retries it — and the retry calls `attemptPublish` again, which re-runs `changeset publish` from the top:

```js
for (let i = 1; i <= MAX_RETRIES; i++) {
  const { success, error } = await attemptPublish(/* ... */);
  if (success) { /* ... */ return; }
  if (error?.nonRetryable) { /* fail fast */ }
  if (i < MAX_RETRIES) await sleep(RETRY_DELAY);
}
```

## Why 2 seconds is not enough

npm serves package metadata from a CDN with read replicas. Propagation after a successful publish is routinely a few seconds and occasionally far longer; it is not bounded by any published SLA. A single check at a fixed offset therefore samples a race. The downstream occurrence verified ~0.3s after publishing and got an E404 for a package that had, in fact, just been published successfully.

## Sequence observed downstream

```
attempt 1 -> changeset publish  -> success
             verify             -> E404 (registry had not propagated)
attempt 2 -> changeset publish  -> "cannot publish over the previously published versions"
attempt 3 -> nothing left to publish; verification finally passed
```

The run went green, but only because attempt 3 landed inside `MAX_RETRIES`. With one more second of propagation lag the same successful publish would have reported a hard failure.

## Reproduction

Deterministic, no registry needed — inject a runner whose publish always succeeds and whose verification misses the first two times:

```js
let publishes = 0;
let checks = 0;
const runner = async cmd => {
  if (cmd.includes('changeset publish')) {
    publishes++;
    if (publishes > 1) throw new Error('cannot publish over the previously published versions');
    return { code: 0, stdout: 'success', stderr: '' };
  }
  // npm view: miss twice, then hit — i.e. normal propagation lag
  if (++checks < 3) throw new Error('npm error code E404');
  return { code: 0, stdout: '1.0.0', stderr: '' };
};
```

Expected: one publish, verification eventually succeeds, exit 0.
Actual: a second `changeset publish` is attempted and fails on the conflict.

## Workaround

Raise `sleep(2000)` before verification. This only widens the race window — it does not remove it, and it slows every successful release to pay for a rare one.

## Suggested fix

Separate the two failure domains. A publish that reported success must never be repeated; the only correct response to a verification miss is **to look again**.

1. Replace the single check with bounded polling (exponential backoff, e.g. 7 attempts from 2s up to 30s).
2. If polling is exhausted, fail the release — but do **not** re-enter the publish path. Return a failure that the retry loop will not act on by republishing.
3. Retry `changeset publish` only when the publish command itself failed.
4. Treat `EPUBLISHCONFLICT` / "already published" as a cue to verify, not to fail.

Reference implementation, now in `link-assistant/hive-mind` at `scripts/publish-to-npm.mjs`: `waitForVersionOnRegistry()` for the polling, and `publishWithRetry()` for the split between the two domains. It keeps the "never trust the publish command alone" property — a publish that falsely claims success still fails the release, because verification is still required.

I'm happy to open a PR porting that structure here if it's welcome.

## Summary

`merge-changesets` skips a malformed changeset with only a `console.warn`, so its bump and release notes silently vanish from the merged release instead of failing loudly.

## Location

`scripts/merge-changesets.mjs:131-133` and `148-149`

```js
console.warn(
  `Warning: Could not parse version type from ${filePath}, skipping`
);
...
} catch (error) {
  console.warn(`Warning: Failed to parse ${filePath}: ${error.message}`);
}
```

## Reproduction

Two changesets, one with broken frontmatter (e.g. a typo'd bump key). The valid one merges; the broken one is skipped with a warning. The release ships **without** the dropped changeset's bump/notes, and CI stays green — the loss is invisible unless someone reads the logs.

## Suggested fix

Treat an unparseable changeset as a hard error (non-zero exit) so a malformed file blocks the release rather than being silently dropped — or at least surface it via `::error::` / a failing check, not `console.warn`.

## Notes

Found while auditing `link-assistant/web-search` (issue #17); the same behavior exists there.

## Problem

`scripts/create-github-release.mjs` hardcodes `v${version}` as the GitHub release tag:

```js
// Line 53 of scripts/create-github-release.mjs
const tag = `v${version}`;
```

Similarly, `scripts/format-github-release.mjs` hardcodes the same pattern:

```js
// Line 57 of scripts/format-github-release.mjs
const tag = `v${version}`;
```

When this template is used in a **multi-language repository** (e.g., one repo with both a JS and a Rust package), tag names collide or are ambiguous:

- `v1.7.8` — is this a JS release or a Rust release?

The Rust equivalent in the Rust template uses `rust-v<version>` to be explicit.

## Workaround

In downstream repos, manually change the tag to `js-v${version}` in both scripts. This is what was done in [link-assistant/web-capture#89](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-capture/pull/89) to fix [issue #88](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-capture/issues/88).

## Suggested Fix

Change `scripts/create-github-release.mjs`:

```js
// Before:
const tag = `v${version}`;
name: version,

// After:
const tag = `js-v${version}`;
name: `JS v${version}`,
```

Change `scripts/format-github-release.mjs`:

```js
// Before:
const tag = `v${version}`;

// After:
const tag = `js-v${version}`;
```

Or better, add a `--tag-prefix` CLI parameter (defaulting to `v`) so template users can override it without modifying the script:

```js
.option('tag-prefix', {
  type: 'string',
  default: getenv('TAG_PREFIX', 'v'),
  describe: 'Prefix for the git tag (e.g., "js-v" for multi-language repos)',
})
// ...
const tag = `${tagPrefix}${version}`;
```

And pass it from the workflow:

```yaml
run: node scripts/create-github-release.mjs --release-version "..." --repository "..." --tag-prefix "js-"
```

## Reproducible Example

1. Clone this template.
2. Set up a repo that also has a Rust package using the Rust template.
3. Trigger a JS release — tag will be `v1.0.0`.
4. Trigger a Rust release — tag will be `rust-v1.0.0`.
5. The JS release tag `v1.0.0` is ambiguous; should be `js-v1.0.0`.

## Problem

The `scripts/detect-code-changes.mjs` script compares `GITHUB_BASE_SHA...GITHUB_HEAD_SHA` for pull request events, which returns **all files changed across the entire PR**, not just what the latest push changed.

This means a commit that only modifies non-code files (e.g. `.gitkeep`, `README.md`) will still trigger all CI jobs if any earlier commit in the same PR touched code files.

### Evidence

In [link-assistant/web-capture PR #49](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-capture/pull/49), commit `0e9b6e8c` only modified `.gitkeep` but triggered all 8 CI jobs (lint, test on 3 OSes, build, changeset check) because the PR as a whole contained code changes.

### Root Cause

GitHub Actions checks out a **synthetic merge commit** for \`pull_request\` events:
- \`HEAD\` = merge commit
- \`HEAD^\` = base branch (first parent)
- \`HEAD^2\` = actual PR head commit (second parent)

Using \`git diff HEAD^ HEAD\` gives the full PR diff (base branch → merge), not the per-commit diff.

## Suggested Fix

Detect the merge commit and use \`HEAD^2^..HEAD^2\` to get the per-commit diff:

\`\`\`javascript
function isMergeCommit() {
  const parentCount = exec('git cat-file -p HEAD')
    .split('\n')
    .filter((line) => line.startsWith('parent ')).length;
  return parentCount > 1;
}

function getChangedFiles() {
  if (isMergeCommit()) {
    console.log('Merge commit detected (pull_request event)');
    console.log('Comparing HEAD^2^ to HEAD^2 (per-commit diff of PR head)');
    try {
      const output = exec('git diff --name-only HEAD^2^ HEAD^2');
      return output ? output.split('\n').filter(Boolean) : [];
    } catch {
      // First commit in PR — fall back to full diff against base
      const output = exec('git diff --name-only HEAD^ HEAD^2');
      return output ? output.split('\n').filter(Boolean) : [];
    }
  }

  // Push events: HEAD is the real commit
  console.log('Comparing HEAD^ to HEAD');
  try {
    const output = exec('git diff --name-only HEAD^ HEAD');
    return output ? output.split('\n').filter(Boolean) : [];
  } catch {
    const output = exec('git ls-tree --name-only -r HEAD');
    return output ? output.split('\n').filter(Boolean) : [];
  }
}
\`\`\`

### Workaround

The fix has been implemented and verified in CI in [link-assistant/web-capture PR #51](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-capture/pull/51).

Related issue: https://github.com/link-assistant/web-capture/issues/50

```
      // Code quality rules
      'no-unused-vars': [
        'error',
        {
          argsIgnorePattern: '^_',
          varsIgnorePattern: '^_',
          caughtErrorsIgnorePattern: '^_',
        },
      ],
```

This must be without any exceptions at all by design. Make sure it is mentioned at README.md. Also make sure README.md correctly reflects current state of code.

## Bug Description

The `scripts/format-release-notes.mjs` script only handles `### Patch Changes` sections, causing it to fail on Minor and Major releases.

## Current Behavior

When a Minor or Major release is created:
- ❌ Section headers ("### Minor Changes", "### Major Changes") remain in release notes
- ❌ PR detection is skipped
- ❌ Release formatting fails silently

**Example:** Release v0.1.0 shows:
```
### Minor Changes

- 65d76dc: Initial template setup with complete AI-driven development pipeline
  ...
```

## Expected Behavior

Release notes should be formatted cleanly:
```
Initial template setup with complete AI-driven development pipeline
...

**Related Pull Request:** #X

---

[![npm version](https://img.shields.io/badge/npm-0.1.0-blue.svg)](...)
```

## Root Cause

File: `scripts/format-release-notes.mjs` (lines 92-115)

```javascript
// Current code - only matches Patch changes
const patchChangesMatchWithHash = currentBody.match(
  /### Patch Changes\s*\n\s*-\s+([a-f0-9]+):\s+(.+?)$/s
);
const patchChangesMatchNoHash = currentBody.match(
  /### Patch Changes\s*\n\s*-\s+(.+?)$/s
);
```

When the regex doesn't match:
1. Script exits early with "⚠️ Could not parse patch changes"
2. Section header remains in release notes
3. PR detection is never executed

## Proposed Solution

Update regex to match all changeset types:

```javascript
// Match any changeset type (Major, Minor, or Patch)
const changesPattern =
  /### (Major|Minor|Patch) Changes\s*\n\s*-\s+(?:([a-f0-9]+):\s+)?(.+?)$/s;
const changesMatch = currentBody.match(changesPattern);

let commitHash = null;
let rawDescription = null;
let changeType = null;

if (changesMatch) {
  [, changeType, commitHash, rawDescription] = changesMatch;
  console.log(`ℹ️ Found ${changeType} Changes section`);

  // Extract commit hash if embedded in description
  if (!commitHash && rawDescription) {
    const descWithHashMatch = rawDescription.match(/^([a-f0-9]+):\s+(.+)$/s);
    if (descWithHashMatch) {
      [, commitHash, rawDescription] = descWithHashMatch;
    }
  }
} else {
  console.log('⚠️ Could not parse changes from release notes');
  console.log('   Looking for pattern: ### [Major|Minor|Patch] Changes');
  process.exit(0);
}
```

## Fix Implementation

We've implemented this fix in our repository:
- **Repository:** link-assistant/agent
- **PR:** https://github.com/link-assistant/agent/pull/59
- **Case Study:** docs/case-studies/issue-58/README.md

The fix:
1. ✅ Matches all changeset types (Major, Minor, Patch)
2. ✅ Removes section headers from release notes
3. ✅ Enables PR detection for all release types
4. ✅ Handles both commit hash formats

## Impact

**Affected Releases:**
- All Minor and Major releases (v0.1.0, etc.)

**Unaffected:**
- Patch releases (these work correctly)

## References

- **Discovered in:** link-assistant/agent#58
- **Fixed in:** link-assistant/agent#59
- **Changeset docs:** https://github.com/changesets/changesets

---

This issue was discovered through comprehensive case study analysis documented in our repository. Happy to contribute the fix back to the template if needed!
